Here is a **complete SSH Local Port Forwarding guide (Basic → Advanced)** with clear descriptions and real use cases.

---

# 🔐 SSH LOCAL PORT FORWARDING (BASIC → ADVANCED)

---

# 🟢 BASIC LEVEL

## 1. Basic local port forwarding

```bash id="b1"
ssh -L 9090:localhost:8090 user@SERVER_IP
```

### Meaning:

* `9090` → your local port
* `localhost:8090` → service on remote server
* `user@SERVER_IP` → SSH login

### Use case:

👉 Access remote web app locally

```text id="b2"
Browser → localhost:9090 → SSH → server:8090
```

---

## 2. Database access (PostgreSQL)

```bash id="b3"
ssh -L 15432:localhost:5432 user@SERVER_IP
```

### Use case:

👉 Connect remote DB like local DB

```bash id="b4"
psql -h localhost -p 15432 -U postgres
```

---

# 🟡 INTERMEDIATE LEVEL

## 3. Run tunnel in background

```bash id="i1"
ssh -f -N -L 9090:localhost:8090 user@SERVER_IP
```

### Flags:

* `-f` → background
* `-N` → no shell
* `-L` → forwarding

### Use case:

👉 Keep tunnel running without terminal

---

## 4. Use different local port (avoid conflict)

```bash id="i2"
ssh -L 8088:localhost:8090 user@SERVER_IP
```

### Use case:

👉 If 9090 already used

---

## 5. Forward multiple ports (same session)

```bash id="i3"
ssh -L 9090:localhost:80 \
    -L 15432:localhost:5432 user@SERVER_IP
```

### Use case:

👉 Access web + DB together

---

# 🔵 ADVANCED LEVEL

## 6. Bind to all interfaces (LAN sharing)

```bash id="a1"
ssh -L 0.0.0.0:9090:localhost:8090 user@SERVER_IP
```

### Use case:

👉 Others in same network can access your tunnel

```text id="a2"
http://YOUR_IP:9090
```

⚠️ Security risk if exposed publicly

---

## 7. Forward to another internal machine (jump scenario)

```bash id="a3"
ssh -L 9000:192.168.1.10:80 user@SERVER_IP
```

### Use case:

👉 Server acts as gateway to internal network

---

## 8. SOCKS proxy (advanced alternative)

```bash id="a4"
ssh -D 1080 user@SERVER_IP
```

### Use case:

👉 Full network proxy (not just one port)

---

## 9. Persistent tunnel (production style)

```bash id="a5"
autossh -M 0 -f -N -L 15432:localhost:5432 user@SERVER_IP
```

### Use case:

👉 Auto-reconnect tunnel in production

---

# 🔄 HOW IT WORKS (ALL CASES)

```text id="flow"
Local Machine Port
        ↓
SSH Encrypted Tunnel (port 22)
        ↓
Remote Server (localhost / internal service)
```

---

# 🔐 SECURITY SUMMARY

✔ No public DB exposure
✔ Only SSH (22) needed
✔ Fully encrypted
✔ Temporary access
✔ Widely used in production

---

# ❌ COMMON MISTAKES

* Using `-l` instead of `-L`
* Thinking forwarded port must be open on server
* Opening DB ports publicly (unsafe)
* Not realizing `localhost` means *remote server side*

---

# 🏁 FINAL ONE-LINE RULE

```text id="final"
SSH -L = Secure tunnel from your local machine → remote service → localhost access
```

